PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L12U1.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":12,"cefr":"C2","unit":1,"theme":"Identity in a Global Age","essential_question":"Can we belong to more than one place without losing who we are?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1","title":"This Much Is Clear; Little Did They Expect: Fronting and Inversion for Emphasis","objectives":["Students will be able to front objects and complements for emphasis and contrast without inversion.","Students will be able to combine fronting with inversion after so, such and negative or restrictive expressions in sophisticated writing.","I can write \"What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging.\"","I can write \"Rarely, if ever, have heritage and technology been so closely connected.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تقديم المفعول به والمكمّل للتوكيد والمقابلة دون قلب.","سيتمكن الطلاب من الجمع بين التقديم والقلب بعد so وsuch والتعبيرات المنفية والمقيِّدة في الكتابة الرفيعة.","أستطيع أن أكتب What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging.","أستطيع أن أكتب Rarely, if ever, have heritage and technology been so closely connected."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"belonging","pos":"noun","ipa":"/bɪˈlɔŋɪŋ/","definition":"the feeling of being accepted as part of a group or place","arabic":"الانتماء","example":"Sports clubs give young people a sense of belonging.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-W01"},{"word":"assimilate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əˈsɪməˌleɪt/","definition":"to become part of a group or culture by adopting its customs","arabic":"يندمج / يستوعب","example":"Immigrants often assimilate within two generations.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-W02"},{"word":"cosmopolitan","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌkɑzməˈpɑlətən/","definition":"including people and influences from many countries","arabic":"عالمي الطابع / متعدد الثقافات","example":"Dubai is a cosmopolitan city.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-W03"},{"word":"ambivalent","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/æmˈbɪvələnt/","definition":"having mixed or opposite feelings about something","arabic":"متردد / متضارب المشاعر","example":"She felt ambivalent about moving abroad.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Fronting means moving a word or phrase to the start of a sentence for emphasis or contrast. With an object or complement, there is usually no inversion: This much I know. That decision I will never forget. What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging. Strange it may seem, but it's true. (= It may seem strange, but…) This style is literary and rhetorical.\nWith so + adjective / adverb and such + be, invert: So deeply had they absorbed new ideas that home felt strange. Such is the pace of change that old answers no longer work. With negative / restrictive expressions, invert: Little did they expect…; Not once did she complain; Nowhere else is the change so visible; Rarely, if ever, have heritage and technology been so connected; Not since the 1970s had so many students gone abroad; In no way does this mean…\nA fronted comparative or adjective phrase + be takes inversion of the verb be: More important than wealth is a sense of belonging. Equally strong is the opposite trend. Don't invert after a fronted object: (not: This much do I know) → This much I know. And don't forget the auxiliary after negative fronting: (not: Little they expected) → Little did they expect.\nfronted object / complement → no inversion (This much I know.)\nSo + adj/adv + auxiliary + subject… that • Such + be + subject + that\nLittle / Not once / Nowhere / Rarely, if ever / Not since / In no way + auxiliary + subject\nfronted comparative / adjective + be + subject (More important is…)","examples":["This much I know: home is where your family is.","What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging.","So deeply had they absorbed new ideas that home felt strange.","Such is the pace of change that old answers no longer work.","Little did they expect that coming home would be difficult.","Rarely, if ever, have heritage and technology been so connected.","Not since the 1970s had so many students gone abroad.","More important than wealth is a sense of belonging."],"common_errors":["✗ This much do I know. → ✓ This much I know. (No inversion after a fronted object.)","✗ Little they expected that coming home would be hard. → ✓ Little did they expect that coming home would be hard. (Negative fronting → inversion.)","✗ So deeply they had absorbed new ideas that home felt strange. → ✓ So deeply had they absorbed new ideas that home felt strange. (So + adverb → inversion.)","✗ More important than wealth a sense of belonging is. → ✓ More important than wealth is a sense of belonging. (Fronted comparative + be + subject.)"],"arabic":"التقديم هو نقل كلمة أو عبارة إلى أول الجملة للتوكيد أو المقابلة، ومع المفعول به أو المكمّل لا يوجد قلب غالبًا: This much I know.\nمع so + صفة/ظرف وsuch + be نقلب: So deeply had they absorbed… that…، Such is the pace of change that…\nمع التعبيرات المنفية والمقيِّدة نقلب: Little did they expect، Not once did she…، Rarely, if ever, have…، Not since… had…، In no way does…\nمع المقارنة المقدّمة نقلب be: More important than wealth is a sense of belonging."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “This Much Is Clear; Little Did They Expect: Fronting and Inversion for Emphasis”. Let’s learn fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U1-LS1 | This Much Is Clear; Little Did They Expect: Fronting and Inversion for Emphasis","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “Who Am I in a Connected World?”: Gone are the days when most people lived their whole lives within a few kilometers of where they were born. Today, millions of young people study, work and form friendships across borders, and such is the pace of change that the question \"Where are you from?\" no longer has a simple answer. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"Gone are the days when most people lived their whole lives within a few kilometers of where they were born. Today, millions of young people study, work and form friendships across borders, and such is the pace of change that the question \"Where are you from?\" no longer has a simple answer.","visual":"Image GV-L12-U1-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Fronting means moving a word or phrase to the start of a sentence for emphasis or contrast. With an object or complement, there is usually no inversion: This much I know. That decision I will never forget. What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging. Strange it may seem, but it's true. (= It may seem strange, but…) This style is literary and rhetorical. With so + adjective / adverb and such + be, invert: So deeply had they absorbed new ideas that home felt strange. Such is the pace of change that old answers no longer work. With negative / restrictive expressions, invert: Little did they expect…; Not once did she complain; Nowhere else is the change so visible; Rarely, if ever, have heritage and technology been so connected; Not since the 1970s had so many students gone abroad; In no way does this mean…","on_screen":"fronted object / complement → no inversion (This much I know.) • So + adj/adv + auxiliary + subject… that • Such + be + subject + that • Little / Not once / Nowhere / Rarely, if ever / Not since / In no way + auxiliary + subject","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. This much I know: home is where your family is. What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging. So deeply had they absorbed new ideas that home felt strange.","on_screen":"This much I know: home is where your family is. / What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging. / So deeply had they absorbed new ideas that home felt strange.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “This much do I know.” That’s not right. We say: “This much I know.” No inversion after a fronted object.","on_screen":"✗ This much do I know.   ✓ This much I know.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"This much I know.\" Is there inversion? … Think! … No – a fronted object doesn't invert. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","التقديم هو نقل كلمة أو عبارة إلى أول الجملة للتوكيد أو المقابلة، ومع المفعول به أو المكمّل لا يوجد قلب غالبًا: This much I know.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: This much do I know. — الصحيح: This much I know.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Who Am I in a Connected World?","body":"Gone are the days when most people lived their whole lives within a few kilometers of where they were born. Today, millions of young people study, work and form friendships across borders, and such is the pace of change that the question \"Where are you from?\" no longer has a simple answer.\nSince 2005, more than a hundred thousand Saudi students have studied abroad through government scholarship programs. Little did many of them expect that coming home would be as challenging as leaving. So deeply had they absorbed new routines and ideas that familiar places sometimes felt strange, a phenomenon known as reverse culture shock.\nParticularly interesting are the experiences of \"third culture kids,\" children who grow up in a country that is not their parents' homeland. The term was coined by the American sociologist Ruth Hill Useem in the 1950s. Such children often feel at home everywhere and nowhere. What they gain in flexibility they sometimes lose in a sense of belonging.\nCritics of globalization fear that local cultures are being homogenized and that the same films, foods and fashions are replacing traditions everywhere. Equally strong, however, is the opposite trend. Across the Kingdom, young people are rediscovering traditional crafts, dialects and poetry, often sharing them online with a global audience. Rarely, if ever, have heritage and technology been so closely connected.\nIdentity, then, is not a fixed possession but a story we continue to write. This much is clear: in a connected world, we can belong to more than one place without betraying any of them.","word_count":258,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"This much ___: home is where your family is.","options":["know I","do I know","I do know it","I know"],"answer":"I know","explanation":"Fronted object or complement (no inversion).","explanation_ar":"تقديم المفعول به."},{"q":"Little ___ that coming home would be as hard as leaving.","options":["expected many of them","did many of them expect","many of them expected","many of them did expect"],"answer":"did many of them expect","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"So deeply ___ new ideas that familiar places felt strange.","options":["they absorbed","had they absorbed","they had absorbed","absorbed they had"],"answer":"had they absorbed","explanation":"So / Such + inversion.","explanation_ar":"So / Such."},{"q":"Rarely, if ever, ___ heritage and technology been so closely connected.","options":["had they","has","have","were"],"answer":"have","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"More important than wealth ___ a sense of belonging.","options":["has","does","it is","is"],"answer":"is","explanation":"Fronted comparative / adjective + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم المقارنة."},{"q":"Not since the oil boom of the 1970s ______ so many young Saudis traveled overseas to study, according to the education ministry.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["had","did","have had","they had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite with the words in brackets at the front.","items":[{"q":"I will never forget that day. (That day)  (fronted object)","options":[],"answer":"That day I will never forget.","explanation":"Fronted object or complement (no inversion).","explanation_ar":"تقديم المفعول به."},{"q":"She never once complained about living abroad. (Not once)  (negative fronting)","options":[],"answer":"Not once did she complain about living abroad.","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"The pace of change is so great that old answers no longer work. (Such)  (Such + be)","options":[],"answer":"Such is the pace of change that old answers no longer work.","explanation":"So / Such + inversion.","explanation_ar":"So / Such."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"This much do I know: our language is part of who we are.","options":[],"answer":"This much I know: our language is part of who we are.","explanation":"This much do I know → This much I know. Fronted object or complement (no inversion).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: This much do I know → This much I know. تقديم المفعول به."},{"q":"Little they knew how much they would miss home.","options":[],"answer":"Little did they know how much they would miss home.","explanation":"Little they knew → Little did they know. Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Little they knew → Little did they know. التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"More valuable than any certificate a strong network is.","options":[],"answer":"More valuable than any certificate is a strong network.","explanation":"a strong network is → is a strong network. Fronted comparative / adjective + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: a strong network is → is a strong network. تقديم المقارنة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write with emphasis.","items":[{"q":"Begin with \"This much…\".","options":[],"answer":"This much is certain: my grandmother's stories shaped who I am.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin with \"What I gained…, I…\".","options":[],"answer":"What I gained in confidence abroad, I lost in my Arabic writing.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin with \"Not once did…\".","options":[],"answer":"Not once did my father forget to call us while he was studying in Canada.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin with \"Nowhere else…\".","options":[],"answer":"Nowhere else have I felt as much at home as in my grandparents' village.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin with \"More important than… is…\".","options":[],"answer":"More important than where you live is how you treat your neighbors.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A reflective paragraph.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 emphatic sentences about a place that is part of your identity.","options":[],"answer":"So strong is my connection to Taif that I visit every summer. Nowhere else do the roses smell as sweet.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence beginning \"Rarely, if ever, …\".","options":[],"answer":"Rarely, if ever, have I met someone who doesn't love their mother's cooking.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with a fronted object for contrast.","options":[],"answer":"Traditional food I love; traditional music I am still learning to appreciate.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Grammar Hot Seat","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["One student from each team sits in the hot seat facing the class.","The teacher shows a cue card behind the student; the team gives a clue using fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) without saying the word.","The student in the hot seat guesses the cue word within 30 seconds.","Teams score 1 point for a correct guess and 1 point for a correct clue sentence. Rotate seats."],"data":[{"label":"This much is clear"},{"label":"What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging"},{"label":"Such is the pace of change that"},{"label":"Little did they expect"},{"label":"Rarely, if ever, have"},{"label":"Gone are the days"},{"label":"Particularly interesting are"},{"label":"Into the room walked"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"That kind of loneliness ___.","options":["I had never felt before","never I had felt before","had I never felt before","I never had felt it before"],"answer":"I had never felt before","explanation":"Fronted object or complement (no inversion).","explanation_ar":"تقديم المفعول به."},{"q":"Not once ___ about the cold weather.","options":["complained he","he did complain","did he complain","he complained"],"answer":"did he complain","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"Such ___ her love of the language that she studied it for ten years.","options":["had","did","were","was"],"answer":"was","explanation":"So / Such + inversion.","explanation_ar":"So / Such."},{"q":"Nowhere else ___ so many cultures living side by side.","options":["you find","will you find","you will find","find you will"],"answer":"will you find","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"Equally important ___ the ability to listen to others.","options":["is","has","does","it is"],"answer":"is","explanation":"Fronted comparative / adjective + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم المقارنة."},{"q":"","options":["In no way does living abroad mean forgetting your roots.","In no way living abroad means forgetting your roots.","In no way means living abroad forgetting your roots.","In no way living abroad does mean forgetting your roots."],"answer":"In no way does living abroad mean forgetting your roots.","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"","options":["What lacked she in experience she made up for in determination.","What she lacked in experience she made it up for in determination.","What she lacked in experience did she make up for in determination.","What she lacked in experience she made up for in determination."],"answer":"What she lacked in experience she made up for in determination.","explanation":"What… they… (fronted clause for contrast).","explanation_ar":"What they gained…"},{"q":"So completely ______ the language of her new country that, within two years, her classmates assumed she had been born there.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["mastered she had","she had mastered","had she mastered","she mastered"],"answer":"had she mastered","explanation":"So / Such + inversion.","explanation_ar":"So / Such."},{"q":"Little ______ that the poems he wrote for his family would one day be translated into twelve languages.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["imagined the young poet","did the young poet imagine","the young poet imagined","the young poet did imagine"],"answer":"did the young poet imagine","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"Why does \"This much I know\" have no inversion?","options":["It is a question.","It is informal.","\"Know\" cannot be inverted.","The fronted phrase is an object, not a negative adverbial."],"answer":"The fronted phrase is an object, not a negative adverbial.","explanation":"Fronted object or complement (no inversion).","explanation_ar":"تقديم المفعول به."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"An airport arrivals hall: a young Saudi graduate returning home with a suitcase covered in stickers from different cities, greeted by family; split image of the same student in a university library abroad and later teaching traditional Sadu weaving on a livestream. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"An airport arrivals hall: a young Saudi graduate returning home with a suitcase covered in stickers from different cities, greeted by family; split image of the same student in a university library abroad and later teaching traditional Sadu weaving on a livestream"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…), with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows belonging (the feeling of being accepted as part of a group or place); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing belonging: the feeling of being accepted as part of a group or place"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows assimilate (to become part of a group or culture by adopting its customs); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing assimilate: to become part of a group or culture by adopting its customs"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows cosmopolitan (including people and influences from many countries); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing cosmopolitan: including people and influences from many countries"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows ambivalent (having mixed or opposite feelings about something); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing ambivalent: having mixed or opposite feelings about something"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Grammar Hot Seat”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Grammar Hot Seat"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-W01","script":"belonging. belonging. Sports clubs give young people a sense of belonging.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-W02","script":"assimilate. assimilate. Immigrants often assimilate within two generations.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-W03","script":"cosmopolitan. cosmopolitan. Dubai is a cosmopolitan city.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-W04","script":"ambivalent. ambivalent. She felt ambivalent about moving abroad.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-T1","script":"Gone are the days when most people lived their whole lives within a few kilometers of where they were born. Today, millions of young people study, work and form friendships across borders, and such is the pace of change that the question \"Where are you from?\" no longer has a simple answer. Since 2005, more than a hundred thousand Saudi students have studied abroad through government scholarship programs. Little did many of them expect that coming home would be as challenging as leaving. So deeply had they absorbed new routines and ideas that familiar places sometimes felt strange, a phenomenon known as reverse culture shock. Particularly interesting are the experiences of \"third culture kids,\" children who grow up in a country that is not their parents' homeland. The term was coined by the American sociologist Ruth Hill Useem in the 1950s. Such children often feel at home everywhere and nowhere. What they gain in flexibility they sometimes lose in a sense of belonging. Critics of globalization fear that local cultures are being homogenized and that the same films, foods and fashions are replacing traditions everywhere. Equally strong, however, is the opposite trend. Across the Kingdom, young people are rediscovering traditional crafts, dialects and poetry, often sharing them online with a global audience. Rarely, if ever, have heritage and technology been so closely connected. Identity, then, is not a fixed possession but a story we continue to write. This much is clear: in a connected world, we can belong to more than one place without betraying any of them.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “This Much Is Clear; Little Did They Expect: Fronting and Inversion for Emphasis”. Let’s learn fronting without inversion (objects and complements: This much I know; That decision I will never forget; What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging); fronted so / such with inversion (So deeply had they absorbed… that; Such is the pace of change that…); negative and restrictive fronting with inversion (Little did…, Not once did…, Nowhere else…, Rarely, if ever, have…, Not since… had…, In no way…); fronted comparatives (More important than wealth is…) together. Listen to the start of “Who Am I in a Connected World?”: Gone are the days when most people lived their whole lives within a few kilometers of where they were born. Today, millions of young people study, work and form friendships across borders, and such is the pace of change that the question \"Where are you from?\" no longer has a simple answer. Look at the green words. Fronting means moving a word or phrase to the start of a sentence for emphasis or contrast. With an object or complement, there is usually no inversion: This much I know. That decision I will never forget. What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging. Strange it may seem, but it's true. (= It may seem strange, but…) This style is literary and rhetorical. With so + adjective / adverb and such + be, invert: So deeply had they absorbed new ideas that home felt strange. Such is the pace of change that old answers no longer work. With negative / restrictive expressions, invert: Little did they expect…; Not once did she complain; Nowhere else is the change so visible; Rarely, if ever, have heritage and technology been so connected; Not since the 1970s had so many students gone abroad; In no way does this mean… Here are some examples. This much I know: home is where your family is. What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging. So deeply had they absorbed new ideas that home felt strange. Be careful! Some learners say: “This much do I know.” That’s not right. We say: “This much I know.” No inversion after a fronted object. Quick check: \"This much I know.\" Is there inversion? … Think! … No – a fronted object doesn't invert. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"What they gained in flexibility they lost in belonging.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2","title":"Gone Are the Days…, Into the Room Walked…: Fronted Adjectives and Adverbials with Full-Verb Inversion","objectives":["Students will be able to use full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives, participles and adverbials of place and direction.","Students will be able to avoid inversion when the subject is a pronoun.","I can write \"Gone are the days when people stayed in one place.\"","I can write \"Into the room walked a tall stranger\" but \"Into the room he walked\"."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام القلب مع الفعل الرئيس بعد تقديم الصفات واسم المفعول وظروف المكان والاتجاه.","سيتمكن الطلاب من تجنب القلب عندما يكون الفاعل ضميرًا.","أستطيع أن أكتب Gone are the days when people stayed in one place.","أستطيع أن أكتب Into the room walked a tall stranger لكن Into the room he walked."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"hybrid","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈhaɪbrəd/","definition":"mixing two or more different things","arabic":"هجين / مختلط","example":"He grew up with a hybrid identity, part Saudi and part British.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS2-W01"},{"word":"diaspora","pos":"noun","ipa":"/daɪˈæspɚə/","definition":"people who have left their homeland and live in many other countries","arabic":"الشتات / الجاليات في المهجر","example":"The Lebanese diaspora is spread across the world.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS2-W02"},{"word":"nostalgia","pos":"noun","ipa":"/nɔˈstældʒə/","definition":"a feeling of sadness mixed with pleasure when you remember the past","arabic":"الحنين إلى الماضي","example":"The old songs filled him with nostalgia.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS2-W03"},{"word":"authenticity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɔθənˈtɪsɪti/","definition":"the quality of being real and true to its origins","arabic":"الأصالة","example":"Tourists value the authenticity of local food.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"In formal and literary English, we can put an adjective, participle or adverbial first and then invert the subject and the whole verb. Gone are the days when people lived in one place. Particularly interesting are the experiences of third culture kids. Equally important is the role of language. Included in the price is a guided tour. Attached is a copy of my certificate.\nAfter fronted adverbials of place or direction, with intransitive verbs like be, stand, lie, sit, come, go, walk, run, live: On the hill stands the oldest house in the village. Into the room walked a tall stranger. Down came the rain. Here comes the bus! There goes my chance. This creates suspense or drama, often with a long or new subject.\nPronoun subjects do not invert: Here it comes! (NOT (not: Here comes it)) Into the room he walked. Down it came. Note the difference from negative fronting: after Never, Little, Rarely we use an auxiliary (Never have I seen…); after fronted place adverbials and adjectives, the main verb or be moves before the subject (On the hill stands a house), and do is not added.\nGone are… • Particularly / Equally / Especially + adjective + be + subject\nIncluded / Attached / Enclosed + be + subject\nplace / direction adverbial + intransitive verb + noun subject (Into the room walked…)\npronoun subject → no inversion (Here it comes.)","examples":["Gone are the days when people stayed in one place all their lives.","Particularly interesting are the experiences of third culture kids.","Equally important is the role of the mother tongue.","On the hill stands the oldest house in the village.","Into the room walked a tall stranger in a white thobe.","Here comes our grandfather with his stories!","Attached is a copy of my scholarship letter.","Here it comes! (NOT (not: Here comes it))"],"common_errors":["✗ Gone the days are when people wrote letters. → ✓ Gone are the days when people wrote letters. (Gone + be + subject.)","✗ Here comes it! → ✓ Here it comes! (Pronoun subject → no inversion.)","✗ On the hill does stand the oldest house. → ✓ On the hill stands the oldest house. (No do in full-verb inversion.)","✗ Particularly interesting the stories are of the elders. → ✓ Particularly interesting are the stories of the elders. (Adjective + be + subject.)"],"arabic":"في الأسلوب الرسمي والأدبي يمكن تقديم صفة أو اسم مفعول أو ظرف ثم قلب الفاعل مع الفعل كاملًا: Gone are the days when…، Particularly interesting are…، Attached is…\nبعد ظروف المكان والاتجاه مع الأفعال اللازمة: On the hill stands the oldest house، Into the room walked a tall stranger، Here comes the bus.\nإذا كان الفاعل ضميرًا لا يحدث قلب: Here it comes / Into the room he walked.\nالفرق عن التقديم المنفي: لا نضيف do هنا، بل ينتقل الفعل الرئيس أو be قبل الفاعل."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Gone Are the Days…, Into the Room Walked…: Fronted Adjectives and Adverbials with Full-Verb Inversion”. Let’s learn full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U1-LS2 | Gone Are the Days…, Into the Room Walked…: Fronted Adjectives and Adverbials with Full-Verb Inversion","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: Gone are the days of waiting weeks for a letter. (Full-verb inversion is used in positive statements.) (Full-verb inversion is used in statements, not questions.)","on_screen":"Gone are the days of waiting weeks for a letter. | (Full-verb inversion is used in positive statements.) | (Full-verb inversion is used in statements, not questions.)","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"In formal and literary English, we can put an adjective, participle or adverbial first and then invert the subject and the whole verb. Gone are the days when people lived in one place. Particularly interesting are the experiences of third culture kids. Equally important is the role of language. Included in the price is a guided tour. Attached is a copy of my certificate. After fronted adverbials of place or direction, with intransitive verbs like be, stand, lie, sit, come, go, walk, run, live: On the hill stands the oldest house in the village. Into the room walked a tall stranger. Down came the rain. Here comes the bus! There goes my chance. This creates suspense or drama, often with a long or new subject.","on_screen":"Gone are… • Particularly / Equally / Especially + adjective + be + subject • Included / Attached / Enclosed + be + subject • place / direction adverbial + intransitive verb + noun subject (Into the room walked…)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Gone are the days when people stayed in one place all their lives. Particularly interesting are the experiences of third culture kids. Equally important is the role of the mother tongue.","on_screen":"Gone are the days when people stayed in one place all their lives. / Particularly interesting are the experiences of third culture kids. / Equally important is the role of the mother tongue.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Gone the days are when people wrote letters.” That’s not right. We say: “Gone are the days when people wrote letters.” Gone + be + subject.","on_screen":"✗ Gone the days are when people wrote letters.   ✓ Gone are the days when people wrote letters.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Here comes it\" or \"Here it comes\"? … Think! … *Here it comes.* Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Gone are the days when people stayed in one place.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Gone are the days when people stayed in one place.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","في الأسلوب الرسمي والأدبي يمكن تقديم صفة أو اسم مفعول أو ظرف ثم قلب الفاعل مع الفعل كاملًا: Gone are the days when…، Particularly interesting are…، Attached is…","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Gone the days are when people wrote letters. — الصحيح: Gone are the days when people wrote letters.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Gone are the days when people stayed in one place."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"___ the days when most people lived in one village all their lives.","options":["Gone is","Are gone","Gone are","The gone are"],"answer":"Gone are","explanation":"Gone are / Gone is…","explanation_ar":"Gone are the days."},{"q":"Particularly interesting ___ the experiences of third culture kids.","options":["are","have","is","do"],"answer":"are","explanation":"Fronted adjective + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم الصفة."},{"q":"On the hill ___ the oldest house in the village.","options":["it stands","does stand","standing","stands"],"answer":"stands","explanation":"Place / direction adverbial + verb + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم ظرف المكان."},{"q":"The bus is coming! Look, here ___!","options":["comes it","does it come","it does come","it comes"],"answer":"it comes","explanation":"No inversion with pronoun subjects.","explanation_ar":"الضمير لا يُقلب."},{"q":"___ is a copy of my scholarship letter.","options":["Attaching","Attached","To attach","Attach"],"answer":"Attached","explanation":"Included / Attached / Enclosed + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم اسم المفعول."},{"q":"Behind the old mosque, hidden among palm trees, ______ that has belonged to the same family for five generations.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["does lie a small garden","lies a small garden","a small garden lies it","lying a small garden"],"answer":"lies a small garden","explanation":"Place / direction adverbial + verb + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم ظرف المكان."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite with the words in brackets at the front.","items":[{"q":"A tall stranger walked into the room. (Into the room)  (full-verb inversion)","options":[],"answer":"Into the room walked a tall stranger.","explanation":"Place / direction adverbial + verb + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم ظرف المكان."},{"q":"The days when people waited weeks for a letter are gone. (Gone)  (Gone are)","options":[],"answer":"Gone are the days when people waited weeks for a letter.","explanation":"Gone are / Gone is…","explanation_ar":"Gone are the days."},{"q":"A guided tour of the old town is included in the price. (Included)  (participle fronting)","options":[],"answer":"Included in the price is a guided tour of the old town.","explanation":"Included / Attached / Enclosed + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم اسم المفعول."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"Here comes it! The parade is starting.","options":[],"answer":"Here it comes! The parade is starting.","explanation":"Here comes it → Here it comes. No inversion with pronoun subjects.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Here comes it → Here it comes. الضمير لا يُقلب."},{"q":"On the corner does stand a famous old bakery.","options":[],"answer":"On the corner stands a famous old bakery.","explanation":"does stand → stands. Place / direction adverbial + verb + subject.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: does stand → stands. تقديم ظرف المكان."},{"q":"Equally important the role is of the mother tongue.","options":[],"answer":"Equally important is the role of the mother tongue.","explanation":"the role is → is the role. Fronted adjective + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: the role is → is the role. تقديم الصفة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write descriptive and emphatic sentences.","items":[{"q":"Begin with \"Gone are the days…\".","options":[],"answer":"Gone are the days when we had to wait weeks for photos to be printed.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin with \"Particularly… is / are…\".","options":[],"answer":"Particularly memorable are the evenings we spent at my grandfather's farm.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a place with \"In / On / At… stands / lies…\".","options":[],"answer":"In the center of the old town stands a clock tower built of stone.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a dramatic sentence with \"Into… walked / ran…\".","options":[],"answer":"Into the classroom ran a small cat, followed by the school guard.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"Here comes…\" and one with \"Here it comes\".","options":[],"answer":"Here comes the bride! Here she comes now.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A short narrative.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences opening a story about returning home after years abroad, using full-verb inversion.","options":[],"answer":"Up the dusty road drove an old taxi. Out of it stepped a young man carrying a suitcase covered in stickers.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence beginning \"Gone are…\" about your childhood.","options":[],"answer":"Gone are the afternoons when we played football in the street until sunset.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with a pronoun subject to show the difference.","options":[],"answer":"Down the hill he ran, laughing.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Sentence Builder Race","mechanic":"sentence_builder","rules":["Each team receives the same tile set.","In 5 minutes, build as many different correct sentences as possible using full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came).","Write each sentence on the team sheet.","Swap sheets with another team to check. One point per correct sentence; no points for repeats."],"data":[{"cue":"this much / be / clear (fronted complement)"},{"cue":"they / gain flexibility / lose belonging (What… they…)"},{"cue":"the pace of change / be / so great / that (Such is…)"},{"cue":"they / little / expect (Little did…)"},{"cue":"heritage and technology / rarely, if ever / be so connected"},{"cue":"the days / when / people / stay in one place / be gone"},{"cue":"the experiences of third culture kids / be / particularly interesting"},{"cue":"the new student / walk / into the room (Into the room…)"},{"cue":"here / the bus / come"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___ the times when families gathered every evening around one television.","options":["Gone are","Are gone","Gone is","It's gone"],"answer":"Gone are","explanation":"Gone are / Gone is…","explanation_ar":"Gone are the days."},{"q":"Equally striking ___ the colors of the traditional costumes.","options":["are","has","does","is"],"answer":"are","explanation":"Fronted adjective + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم الصفة."},{"q":"Enclosed ___ two photographs of the house.","options":["has","do","are","is"],"answer":"are","explanation":"Included / Attached / Enclosed + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم اسم المفعول."},{"q":"Out of the old wooden box ___ a bundle of letters.","options":["did fall","fell","it fell","falling"],"answer":"fell","explanation":"Place / direction adverbial + verb + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم ظرف المكان."},{"q":"The rain started suddenly, and down ___ for three hours.","options":["came it","did it come","it came","it did come"],"answer":"it came","explanation":"No inversion with pronoun subjects.","explanation_ar":"الضمير لا يُقلب."},{"q":"","options":["Into the hall did walk the guest of honor.","Into the hall walking the guest of honor.","Into the hall the guest of honor did walk.","Into the hall walked the guest of honor."],"answer":"Into the hall walked the guest of honor.","explanation":"Place / direction adverbial + verb + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم ظرف المكان."},{"q":"","options":["Here she comes with the coffee!","Here does she come with the coffee!","Here she does come with the coffee!","Here comes she with the coffee!"],"answer":"Here she comes with the coffee!","explanation":"No inversion with pronoun subjects.","explanation_ar":"الضمير لا يُقلب."},{"q":"Among the most valuable items in the museum's new collection ______ a handwritten letter from the founder of the city's first school.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["does","it is","are","is"],"answer":"is","explanation":"Fronted adjective + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم الصفة."},{"q":"At the top of the mountain, surrounded by juniper trees, ______ a small village whose stone houses are painted in bright colors.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["sitting","sits","sit","does sit"],"answer":"sits","explanation":"Place / direction adverbial + verb + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم ظرف المكان."},{"q":"Why is \"Here comes it\" incorrect?","options":["\"Here\" cannot start a sentence.","Pronoun subjects do not invert.","It needs \"does\".","\"Comes\" is the wrong tense."],"answer":"Pronoun subjects do not invert.","explanation":"No inversion with pronoun subjects.","explanation_ar":"الضمير لا يُقلب."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A storytelling evening in a heritage village: an old stone house on a hill with a sign \"On this hill stands the oldest house in the village\"; a door opening as a guest walks in; a slide \"Gone are the days of letters – here come video calls\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A storytelling evening in a heritage village: an old stone house on a hill with a sign \"On this hill stands the oldest house in the village\"; a door opening as a guest walks in; a slide \"Gone are the days of letters – here come video calls\""},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came): a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Identity in a Global Age”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows hybrid (mixing two or more different things); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing hybrid: mixing two or more different things"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows diaspora (people who have left their homeland and live in many other countries); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing diaspora: people who have left their homeland and live in many other countries"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows nostalgia (a feeling of sadness mixed with pleasure when you remember the past); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing nostalgia: a feeling of sadness mixed with pleasure when you remember the past"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows authenticity (the quality of being real and true to its origins); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing authenticity: the quality of being real and true to its origins"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Sentence Builder Race”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Sentence Builder Race"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS2-W01","script":"hybrid. hybrid. He grew up with a hybrid identity, part Saudi and part British.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS2-W02","script":"diaspora. diaspora. The Lebanese diaspora is spread across the world.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS2-W03","script":"nostalgia. nostalgia. The old songs filled him with nostalgia.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS2-W04","script":"authenticity. authenticity. Tourists value the authenticity of local food.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Gone Are the Days…, Into the Room Walked…: Fronted Adjectives and Adverbials with Full-Verb Inversion”. Let’s learn full-verb inversion after fronted elements: Gone are the days when…; fronted adjective / participle + be + subject (Particularly interesting are…, Equally important is…, Included in the price is…, Attached is…); fronted place / direction adverbials + intransitive verb + subject (Into the room walked…, On the hill stands…, Down came the rain, Here comes…, There goes…); no inversion with pronoun subjects (Here it comes; Down it came) together. Remember these patterns: Gone are the days of waiting weeks for a letter. (Full-verb inversion is used in positive statements.) (Full-verb inversion is used in statements, not questions.) In formal and literary English, we can put an adjective, participle or adverbial first and then invert the subject and the whole verb. Gone are the days when people lived in one place. Particularly interesting are the experiences of third culture kids. Equally important is the role of language. Included in the price is a guided tour. Attached is a copy of my certificate. After fronted adverbials of place or direction, with intransitive verbs like be, stand, lie, sit, come, go, walk, run, live: On the hill stands the oldest house in the village. Into the room walked a tall stranger. Down came the rain. Here comes the bus! There goes my chance. This creates suspense or drama, often with a long or new subject. Here are some examples. Gone are the days when people stayed in one place all their lives. Particularly interesting are the experiences of third culture kids. Equally important is the role of the mother tongue. Be careful! Some learners say: “Gone the days are when people wrote letters.” That’s not right. We say: “Gone are the days when people wrote letters.” Gone + be + subject. Quick check: \"Here comes it\" or \"Here it comes\"? … Think! … *Here it comes.* Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Gone are the days when people stayed in one place.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3","title":"Torn Between Two Worlds: Abstract and Idiomatic Vocabulary of Identity","objectives":["Students will be able to discuss identity and belonging with precise abstract vocabulary.","Students will be able to use identity idioms and word families accurately.","I can describe feeling \"torn between two cultures\" or \"like a fish out of water\".","I can use \"nostalgic\", \"authenticity\" and \"ambivalence\" correctly."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من مناقشة الهوية والانتماء بمفردات مجردة دقيقة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام تعابير الهوية وعائلات الكلمات بدقة.","أستطيع أن أصف شعوري بأنني «ممزق بين ثقافتين» أو «كسمكة خارج الماء».","أستطيع أن أستخدم nostalgic وauthenticity وambivalence بشكل صحيح."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"multifaceted","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌmʌltiˈfæsətɪd/","definition":"having many different aspects or sides","arabic":"متعدد الأوجه","example":"Identity is a multifaceted concept.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W01"},{"word":"allegiance","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈlidʒəns/","definition":"loyalty to a country, group or person","arabic":"ولاء","example":"He felt allegiance to both of his countries.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W02"},{"word":"uprooted","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/əˈprutɪd/","definition":"forced to leave the place where you have lived for a long time","arabic":"مقتلع من جذوره","example":"The war left thousands of families uprooted.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W03"},{"word":"homogenize","pos":"verb","ipa":"/hoʊˈmɑdʒəˌnaɪz/","definition":"to make things the same or similar","arabic":"يجانس / يوحّد","example":"Some fear that globalization will homogenize cultures.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W04"},{"word":"juxtapose","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˌdʒʌkstəˈpoʊz/","definition":"to place different things side by side to show contrast","arabic":"يقارن بالتجاور / يضع جنبًا إلى جنب","example":"The exhibition juxtaposes ancient and modern art.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W05"},{"word":"ancestry","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈænsɛstri/","definition":"your family's origins in the past","arabic":"النسب / الأصل","example":"Many people take DNA tests to learn about their ancestry.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W06"},{"word":"conformity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈfɔrməti/","definition":"behavior that follows the rules or customs of a group","arabic":"الامتثال / المسايرة","example":"Teenagers often feel pressure toward conformity.","image_id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Abstract words: belonging (feeling accepted), assimilate (become part of a new culture), cosmopolitan (with influences from many countries), ambivalent (having mixed feelings), hybrid (mixed), diaspora (a people living away from the homeland), nostalgia (longing for the past), authenticity (being real and true to origins), multifaceted (with many sides), allegiance (loyalty), uprooted (forced to leave home), homogenize (make the same), juxtapose (place side by side to contrast), ancestry (family origins), conformity (following group norms).\nIdioms: put down roots (settle in a place); feel like a fish out of water (feel uncomfortable in a new place); the best of both worlds (the advantages of two things); find your feet (become confident in a new situation); be torn between (unable to choose between two things); have a foot in both camps (belong to two groups); get lost in translation (lose meaning when moving between languages or cultures); come full circle (return to where you started).\nWord families: nostalgia (n) → nostalgic (adj) → nostalgically (adv); authentic (adj) → authenticity (n) → authenticate (v); ambivalent (adj) → ambivalence (n); conform (v) → conformity (n) → conformist (n); assimilate (v) → assimilation (n); homogeneous (adj) → homogenize (v) → homogeneity (n). Connotation: assimilate can sound positive (fitting in) or negative (losing your culture); cosmopolitan is usually positive.\nWord families: nostalgia: nostalgic (adj), nostalgically (adv) | authentic: authenticity (n), authenticate (v) | ambivalent: ambivalence (n) | conform: conformity (n), conformist (n) | assimilate: assimilation (n) | homogeneous: homogenize (v), homogeneity (n)\nCollocations/phrases: put down roots • a fish out of water • the best of both worlds • find your feet | torn between two cultures • a foot in both camps • lost in translation • come full circle | a sense of belonging • a hybrid identity • a cosmopolitan city • cultural authenticity | feel ambivalent about • trace your ancestry • pressure toward conformity • juxtapose old and new","examples":["Sports clubs give young people a sense of belonging.","At first, I felt like a fish out of water in my new school.","Living in two countries gives her the best of both worlds.","It took me six months to find my feet at university.","Many children of migrants feel torn between two cultures.","Some jokes get lost in translation.","The old songs make my grandmother feel nostalgic.","The exhibition juxtaposes ancient manuscripts and digital art."],"common_errors":["✗ I felt like a fish out of the sea. → ✓ I felt like a fish out of water. (Fixed idiom.)","✗ She has a nostalgia feeling about her village. → ✓ She feels nostalgic about her village. (Adjective → nostalgic.)","✗ The restaurant is famous for its authentic. → ✓ The restaurant is famous for its authenticity. (Noun → authenticity.)","✗ He felt ambivalent to move abroad. → ✓ He felt ambivalent about moving abroad. (ambivalent about + -ing.)"],"arabic":"كلمات مجردة: belonging (الانتماء) وassimilate (يندمج) وcosmopolitan (متعدد الثقافات) وambivalent (متضارب المشاعر) وhybrid (هجين) وdiaspora (الشتات) وnostalgia (الحنين) وauthenticity (الأصالة) وallegiance (الولاء).\nتعابير: put down roots (يستقر) وa fish out of water (غريب في مكانه) وthe best of both worlds (أفضل ما في العالمين) وfind your feet (يتأقلم) وtorn between (ممزق بين) وlost in translation (يضيع في الترجمة).\nعائلات الكلمات: nostalgia → nostalgic، authentic → authenticity، ambivalent → ambivalence، conform → conformity.\nassimilate قد تحمل دلالة إيجابية (الاندماج) أو سلبية (فقدان الثقافة الأصلية)."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Torn Between Two Worlds: Abstract and Idiomatic Vocabulary of Identity”. Let’s learn abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U1-LS3 | Torn Between Two Worlds: Abstract and Idiomatic Vocabulary of Identity","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. multifaceted: having many different aspects or sides. allegiance: loyalty to a country, group or person. uprooted: forced to leave the place where you have lived for a long time.","on_screen":"multifaceted | allegiance | uprooted","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Abstract words: belonging (feeling accepted), assimilate (become part of a new culture), cosmopolitan (with influences from many countries), ambivalent (having mixed feelings), hybrid (mixed), diaspora (a people living away from the homeland), nostalgia (longing for the past), authenticity (being real and true to origins), multifaceted (with many sides), allegiance (loyalty), uprooted (forced to leave home), homogenize (make the same), juxtapose (place side by side to contrast), ancestry (family origins), conformity (following group norms). Idioms: put down roots (settle in a place); feel like a fish out of water (feel uncomfortable in a new place); the best of both worlds (the advantages of two things); find your feet (become confident in a new situation); be torn between (unable to choose between two things); have a foot in both camps (belong to two groups); get lost in translation (lose meaning when moving between languages or cultures); come full circle (return to where you started).","on_screen":"abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Sports clubs give young people a sense of belonging. At first, I felt like a fish out of water in my new school. Living in two countries gives her the best of both worlds.","on_screen":"Sports clubs give young people a sense of belonging. / At first, I felt like a fish out of water in my new school. / Living in two countries gives her the best of both worlds.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “I felt like a fish out of the sea.” That’s not right. We say: “I felt like a fish out of water.” Fixed idiom.","on_screen":"✗ I felt like a fish out of the sea.   ✓ I felt like a fish out of water.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: If you feel \"like a fish out of water\", are you comfortable? … Think! … No. Great job! Now you can say: I can describe feeling \"torn between two cultures\" or \"like a fish out of water\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can describe feeling \"torn between two cultures\" or \"like a fish out of water\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","كلمات مجردة: belonging (الانتماء) وassimilate (يندمج) وcosmopolitan (متعدد الثقافات) وambivalent (متضارب المشاعر) وhybrid (هجين) وdiaspora (الشتات) وnostalgia (الحنين) وauthenticity (الأصالة) وallegiance (الولاء).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: I felt like a fish out of the sea. — الصحيح: I felt like a fish out of water.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أصف شعوري بأنني «ممزق بين ثقافتين» أو «كسمكة خارج الماء»."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"At first, I felt like a fish out of ___ in my new school abroad.","options":["water","the river","the sea","its bowl"],"answer":"water","explanation":"Identity idiom.","explanation_ar":"التعبير الاصطلاحي."},{"q":"Living in Riyadh and studying in London gives her the best of ___.","options":["all worlds","both worlds","two worlds","both sides of the coin"],"answer":"both worlds","explanation":"Identity idiom.","explanation_ar":"التعبير الاصطلاحي."},{"q":"The old songs make my grandmother feel ___.","options":["nostalgically","nostalgist","nostalgia","nostalgic"],"answer":"nostalgic","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"Many children of migrants feel ___ between two cultures.","options":["torn","pulled out","broken","split up"],"answer":"torn","explanation":"Identity idiom.","explanation_ar":"التعبير الاصطلاحي."},{"q":"He felt ___ about moving abroad: excited, but also afraid.","options":["cosmopolitan","ambivalent","uprooted","homogenized"],"answer":"ambivalent","explanation":"Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The museum's new exhibition ______ ancient Nabataean inscriptions and modern digital artworks, inviting visitors to notice both the differences and the surprising similarities between them.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["uproots","homogenizes","juxtaposes","assimilates"],"answer":"juxtaposes","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"diaspora","options":["become confident in a new situation","people who live in many countries away from their homeland","loyalty to a country, group or person","return to where you started","your family's origins in the past","following the rules or customs of a group"],"answer":"people who live in many countries away from their homeland","explanation":"diaspora → people who live in many countries away from their homeland. Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"allegiance","options":["become confident in a new situation","people who live in many countries away from their homeland","loyalty to a country, group or person","return to where you started","your family's origins in the past","following the rules or customs of a group"],"answer":"loyalty to a country, group or person","explanation":"allegiance → loyalty to a country, group or person. Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"ancestry","options":["become confident in a new situation","people who live in many countries away from their homeland","loyalty to a country, group or person","return to where you started","your family's origins in the past","following the rules or customs of a group"],"answer":"your family's origins in the past","explanation":"ancestry → your family's origins in the past. Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"conformity","options":["become confident in a new situation","people who live in many countries away from their homeland","loyalty to a country, group or person","return to where you started","your family's origins in the past","following the rules or customs of a group"],"answer":"following the rules or customs of a group","explanation":"conformity → following the rules or customs of a group. Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"find your feet","options":["become confident in a new situation","people who live in many countries away from their homeland","loyalty to a country, group or person","return to where you started","your family's origins in the past","following the rules or customs of a group"],"answer":"become confident in a new situation","explanation":"find your feet → become confident in a new situation. Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"come full circle","options":["become confident in a new situation","people who live in many countries away from their homeland","loyalty to a country, group or person","return to where you started","your family's origins in the past","following the rules or customs of a group"],"answer":"return to where you started","explanation":"come full circle → return to where you started. Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Talk about identity.","items":[{"q":"When have you felt like a fish out of water?","options":[],"answer":"I felt like a fish out of water on my first day at a new school.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"What gives you a sense of belonging?","options":[],"answer":"My football team gives me a strong sense of belonging.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe something that got lost in translation.","options":[],"answer":"A joke I told in English got lost in translation when I explained it in Arabic.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"What makes a restaurant or festival feel authentic?","options":[],"answer":"Traditional recipes and local cooks make a restaurant feel authentic.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Do you think globalization will homogenize cultures? Why or why not?","options":[],"answer":"Partly, but many young people are reviving local traditions online.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: An identity profile.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences about your identity using 2 idioms and 1 abstract word.","options":[],"answer":"I sometimes feel torn between two cultures. However, I think I have the best of both worlds. My identity is multifaceted.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences using two forms from the \"authentic\" family.","options":[],"answer":"The guide promised an authentic experience. Visitors valued the authenticity of the village.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"put down roots\".","options":[],"answer":"After years of moving, my family finally put down roots in Jeddah.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Family Tree","mechanic":"drag_and_drop","rules":["Teams take turns drawing a word card.","Place the card on the correct branch of the tree and say a sentence with it.","Correct placement and sentence = 2 points.","The team with the most points when the tree is full wins."],"data":[{"word":"nostalgic","category":"adjective","family":"nostalgia"},{"word":"nostalgically","category":"adverb","family":"nostalgia"},{"word":"authenticity","category":"noun","family":"authentic"},{"word":"authenticate","category":"verb","family":"authentic"},{"word":"ambivalence","category":"noun","family":"ambivalent"},{"word":"conformity","category":"noun","family":"conform"},{"word":"conformist","category":"noun","family":"conform"},{"word":"assimilation","category":"noun","family":"assimilate"},{"word":"homogenize","category":"verb","family":"homogeneous"},{"word":"homogeneity","category":"noun","family":"homogeneous"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"It took me months to ___ at my new university.","options":["get my feet","find my feet","stand my feet","find my legs"],"answer":"find my feet","explanation":"Identity idiom.","explanation_ar":"التعبير الاصطلاحي."},{"q":"Dubai is a ___ city with residents from over 200 countries.","options":["cosmopolitan","ambivalent","homogenized","uprooted"],"answer":"cosmopolitan","explanation":"Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The war left thousands of families ___.","options":["hybrid","assimilated","uprooted","nostalgic"],"answer":"uprooted","explanation":"Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Teenagers often feel pressure toward ___.","options":["conform","conformist","conformable","conformity"],"answer":"conformity","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"The ___ of immigrants into a new society can take several generations.","options":["assimilated","assimilating","assimilation","assimilate"],"answer":"assimilation","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"Her ___ about leaving home was clear: she was both sad and excited.","options":["ambivalently","ambivalence","ambivalents","ambivalent"],"answer":"ambivalence","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"After working abroad for twenty years, he returned to teach at his old school; his life had come ___.","options":["full round","round circle","back circle","full circle"],"answer":"full circle","explanation":"Identity idiom.","explanation_ar":"التعبير الاصطلاحي."},{"q":"Many people now take DNA tests to learn about their ___.","options":["ancestry","conformity","allegiance","diaspora"],"answer":"ancestry","explanation":"Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Because the novel's narrator grew up speaking Arabic at home and English at school, she describes her identity as ______, a mixture of two cultures rather than a choice between them.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["hybrid","uprooted","homogeneous","conformist"],"answer":"hybrid","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"Some critics worry that global media will ______ local cultures, making towns around the world look and sound increasingly alike.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["juxtapose","homogenize","authenticate","assimilate into"],"answer":"homogenize","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A family photo album open on a table: black-and-white photos of grandparents in a village, a color photo of the family abroad, a modern selfie in Riyadh; a word-family tree on the wall: nostalgia → nostalgic → nostalgically. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A family photo album open on a table: black-and-white photos of grandparents in a village, a color photo of the family abroad, a modern selfie in Riyadh; a word-family tree on the wall: nostalgia → nostalgic → nostalgically"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows multifaceted (having many different aspects or sides); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing multifaceted: having many different aspects or sides"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows allegiance (loyalty to a country, group or person); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing allegiance: loyalty to a country, group or person"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows uprooted (forced to leave the place where you have lived for a long time); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing uprooted: forced to leave the place where you have lived for a long time"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows homogenize (to make things the same or similar); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing homogenize: to make things the same or similar"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows juxtapose (to place different things side by side to show contrast); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing juxtapose: to place different things side by side to show contrast"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows ancestry (your family's origins in the past); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing ancestry: your family's origins in the past"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows conformity (behavior that follows the rules or customs of a group); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing conformity: behavior that follows the rules or customs of a group"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Family Tree”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Family Tree"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W01","script":"multifaceted. multifaceted. Identity is a multifaceted concept.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W02","script":"allegiance. allegiance. He felt allegiance to both of his countries.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W03","script":"uprooted. uprooted. The war left thousands of families uprooted.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W04","script":"homogenize. homogenize. Some fear that globalization will homogenize cultures.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W05","script":"juxtapose. juxtapose. The exhibition juxtaposes ancient and modern art.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W06","script":"ancestry. ancestry. Many people take DNA tests to learn about their ancestry.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-W07","script":"conformity. conformity. Teenagers often feel pressure toward conformity.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Torn Between Two Worlds: Abstract and Idiomatic Vocabulary of Identity”. Let’s learn abstract identity vocabulary (belonging, assimilate, cosmopolitan, ambivalent, hybrid, diaspora, nostalgia, authenticity, multifaceted, allegiance, uprooted, homogenize, juxtapose, ancestry, conformity); idioms (put down roots, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds, find your feet, torn between, have a foot in both camps, lost in translation, come full circle); word families (nostalgia → nostalgic; authentic → authenticity; ambivalent → ambivalence; conform → conformity → conformist; assimilate → assimilation; homogeneous → homogenize) together. Meet some new words. multifaceted: having many different aspects or sides. allegiance: loyalty to a country, group or person. uprooted: forced to leave the place where you have lived for a long time. Abstract words: belonging (feeling accepted), assimilate (become part of a new culture), cosmopolitan (with influences from many countries), ambivalent (having mixed feelings), hybrid (mixed), diaspora (a people living away from the homeland), nostalgia (longing for the past), authenticity (being real and true to origins), multifaceted (with many sides), allegiance (loyalty), uprooted (forced to leave home), homogenize (make the same), juxtapose (place side by side to contrast), ancestry (family origins), conformity (following group norms). Idioms: put down roots (settle in a place); feel like a fish out of water (feel uncomfortable in a new place); the best of both worlds (the advantages of two things); find your feet (become confident in a new situation); be torn between (unable to choose between two things); have a foot in both camps (belong to two groups); get lost in translation (lose meaning when moving between languages or cultures); come full circle (return to where you started). Here are some examples. Sports clubs give young people a sense of belonging. At first, I felt like a fish out of water in my new school. Living in two countries gives her the best of both worlds. Be careful! Some learners say: “I felt like a fish out of the sea.” That’s not right. We say: “I felt like a fish out of water.” Fixed idiom. Quick check: If you feel \"like a fish out of water\", are you comfortable? … Think! … No. Great job! Now you can say: I can describe feeling \"torn between two cultures\" or \"like a fish out of water\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS4","title":"Although She Had Lived Abroad, She…: Boundaries in Complex Sentences (SAT)","objectives":["Students will be able to identify independent and dependent clauses in long sentences and choose the correct boundary punctuation.","Students will be able to correct fragments, run-ons and comma splices in complex sentences.","I can write \"Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic.\"","I can spot a fragment such as \"Because her family moved so often.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تحديد الجمل المستقلة والتابعة في الجمل الطويلة واختيار علامة الترقيم الصحيحة عند الحد بينها.","سيتمكن الطلاب من تصحيح أشباه الجمل الناقصة والجمل المتداخلة والربط الخاطئ بالفاصلة في الجمل المعقدة.","أستطيع أن أكتب Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic.","أستطيع أن أتعرّف على الجملة الناقصة مثل Because her family moved so often."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"belonging","pos":"noun","ipa":"/bɪˈlɔŋɪŋ/","definition":"the feeling of being accepted as part of a group or place","arabic":"الانتماء","example":"Sports clubs give young people a sense of belonging.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"assimilate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əˈsɪməˌleɪt/","definition":"to become part of a group or culture by adopting its customs","arabic":"يندمج / يستوعب","example":"Immigrants often assimilate within two generations.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"cosmopolitan","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌkɑzməˈpɑlətən/","definition":"including people and influences from many countries","arabic":"عالمي الطابع / متعدد الثقافات","example":"Dubai is a cosmopolitan city.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"ambivalent","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/æmˈbɪvələnt/","definition":"having mixed or opposite feelings about something","arabic":"متردد / متضارب المشاعر","example":"She felt ambivalent about moving abroad.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"hybrid","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈhaɪbrəd/","definition":"mixing two or more different things","arabic":"هجين / مختلط","example":"He grew up with a hybrid identity, part Saudi and part British.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"diaspora","pos":"noun","ipa":"/daɪˈæspɚə/","definition":"people who have left their homeland and live in many other countries","arabic":"الشتات / الجاليات في المهجر","example":"The Lebanese diaspora is spread across the world.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Start by labeling the clauses on each side of the boundary. An independent clause can stand alone (She still dreamed in Arabic). A dependent clause begins with a subordinator (although, because, when, since, while, if, unless, after, whereas) or a relative word (who, which, that) and cannot stand alone. A dependent clause by itself is a fragment: (not: Because her family moved so often.)\nDependent + independent → comma: Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic. Independent + dependent → usually no comma: She still dreamed in Arabic although she had lived abroad. (A comma is acceptable before although / whereas / while when they show contrast.) Independent + independent → comma + and / but / so / yet / or; or a semicolon; or a colon (if the second explains the first); or a period. Never a comma alone (comma splice) or nothing at all (run-on).\nLong SAT sentences often hide the main verb. Ask: Where is the subject and main verb of each clause? A participle phrase (Having lived in three countries) or a which-clause needs a main clause to attach to. Non-restrictive which-clauses take commas: The house, which her grandfather built in 1960, still stands. SAT tip: if both sides are complete sentences, choose a period, a semicolon, a colon or comma + FANBOYS.\ndependent clause + , + independent clause\nindependent + dependent → usually no comma (comma OK before although / whereas)\nindependent + independent → , and / ; / : / .\na dependent clause or participle phrase alone = fragment","examples":["Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic.","She still dreamed in Arabic although she had lived abroad for years.","Her family moved often; she attended six schools in ten years.","Her family moved often, so she attended six schools.","Her childhood had one constant: her grandmother's weekly phone call.","Having lived in three countries, Lina speaks four languages.","The house, which her grandfather built in 1960, still stands.","Because her family moved so often, she learned to make friends quickly."],"common_errors":["✗ Because her family moved so often. She learned to make friends quickly. → ✓ Because her family moved so often, she learned to make friends quickly. (Fragment → attach it.)","✗ She had lived in Canada, she still felt Saudi. → ✓ She had lived in Canada, but she still felt Saudi. / Although she had lived in Canada, she still felt Saudi. (Comma splice.)","✗ When I returned home everything felt different my friends had changed. → ✓ When I returned home, everything felt different; my friends had changed. (Run-on.)","✗ The village which my father grew up in is near Abha. (one village only, extra information) → ✓ The village, which my father grew up in, is near Abha. (Non-restrictive → commas.)"],"arabic":"نبدأ بتحديد الجمل على جانبي الحد: الجملة المستقلة تقوم بنفسها، والتابعة تبدأ بأداة ربط مثل although / because / when أو باسم موصول، والجملة التابعة وحدها جملة ناقصة.\nالتابعة + المستقلة → فاصلة: Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic. والمستقلة + التابعة → غالبًا بلا فاصلة.\nالمستقلة + المستقلة → فاصلة مع and / but / so أو فاصلة منقوطة أو نقطتان أو نقطة، ولا تكفي الفاصلة وحدها.\nنصيحة SAT: ابحث عن الفاعل والفعل الرئيس في كل جملة، فإذا كان الجانبان جملتين كاملتين اختر نقطة أو فاصلة منقوطة أو نقطتين أو فاصلة مع أداة عطف."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Although She Had Lived Abroad, She…: Boundaries in Complex Sentences (SAT)”. Let’s learn punctuating boundaries in long sentences: introductory dependent clause + comma + main clause; no comma when the dependent clause follows (except contrast with although / whereas); fragments (a dependent clause or participle phrase alone); comma + coordinating conjunction between independent clauses; semicolons and colons between independent clauses; non-restrictive which-clauses; choosing the boundary in SAT questions by identifying clauses on both sides together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U1-LS4 | Although She Had Lived Abroad, She…: Boundaries in Complex Sentences (SAT)","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Start by labeling the clauses on each side of the boundary. An independent clause can stand alone (She still dreamed in Arabic). A dependent clause begins with a subordinator (although, because, when, since, while, if, unless, after, whereas) or a relative word (who, which, that) and cannot stand alone. A dependent clause by itself is a fragment: (not: Because her family moved so often.)","on_screen":"punctuating boundaries in long sentences: introductory dependent clause + comma + main clause; no comma when the dependent clause follows (except contrast with although / whereas); fragments (a dependent clause or participle phrase alone); comma + coordinating conjunction between independent clauses; semicolons and colons between independent clauses; non-restrictive which-clauses; choosing the boundary in SAT questions by identifying clauses on both sides","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Start by labeling the clauses on each side of the boundary. An independent clause can stand alone (She still dreamed in Arabic). A dependent clause begins with a subordinator (although, because, when, since, while, if, unless, after, whereas) or a relative word (who, which, that) and cannot stand alone. A dependent clause by itself is a fragment: (not: Because her family moved so often.) Dependent + independent → comma: Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic. Independent + dependent → usually no comma: She still dreamed in Arabic although she had lived abroad. (A comma is acceptable before although / whereas / while when they show contrast.) Independent + independent → comma + and / but / so / yet / or; or a semicolon; or a colon (if the second explains the first); or a period. Never a comma alone (comma splice) or nothing at all (run-on).","on_screen":"dependent clause + , + independent clause • independent + dependent → usually no comma (comma OK before although / whereas) • independent + independent → , and / ; / : / .","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic. She still dreamed in Arabic although she had lived abroad for years. Her family moved often; she attended six schools in ten years.","on_screen":"Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic. / She still dreamed in Arabic although she had lived abroad for years. / Her family moved often; she attended six schools in ten years.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Because her family moved so often. She learned to make friends quickly.” That’s not right. We say: “Because her family moved so often, she learned to make friends quickly.” Fragment → attach it.","on_screen":"✗ Because her family moved so often. She learned to make friends quickly.   ✓ Because her family moved so often, she learned to make friends quickly.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Because her family moved so often.\" Is this a complete sentence? … Think! … No – it's a fragment. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: punctuating boundaries in long sentences: introductory dependent clause + comma + main clause; no comma when the dependent clause follows (except contrast with although / whereas); fragments (a dependent clause or participle phrase alone); comma + coordinating conjunction between independent clauses; semicolons and colons between independent clauses; non-restrictive which-clauses; choosing the boundary in SAT questions by identifying clauses on both sides.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نبدأ بتحديد الجمل على جانبي الحد: الجملة المستقلة تقوم بنفسها، والتابعة تبدأ بأداة ربط مثل although / because / when أو باسم موصول، والجملة التابعة وحدها جملة ناقصة.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Because her family moved so often. She learned to make friends quickly. — الصحيح: Because her family moved so often, she learned to make friends quickly.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Between Two Homes (editing passage)","body":"When my family moved to Toronto I was eight years old. At first, I felt like a fish out of water, I could not understand the jokes my classmates told. Although I learned English quickly. I missed my grandmother's stories. Our apartment which overlooked a frozen lake was very different from our home in Taif.","word_count":55,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correctly punctuated answer.","items":[{"q":"Although she had lived in Canada for most of her ______ dreamed in Arabic and celebrated every Eid with her cousins in Taif.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["childhood; she still","childhood. She still","childhood she, still","childhood, she still"],"answer":"childhood, she still","explanation":"Dependent clause + comma + main clause.","explanation_ar":"التابعة ثم المستقلة."},{"q":"Her family moved every two years for her father's ______ attended six different schools before she turned twelve.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["work, she","work she","work; she","work, and, she"],"answer":"work; she","explanation":"Joining independent clauses (; : , and).","explanation_ar":"ربط الجمل المستقلة."},{"q":"","options":["Because her family moved so often; she learned to make friends quickly.","Because her family moved so often, she learned to make friends quickly.","Because, her family moved so often she learned to make friends quickly.","Because her family moved so often. She learned to make friends quickly."],"answer":"Because her family moved so often, she learned to make friends quickly.","explanation":"Fragment.","explanation_ar":"الجملة الناقصة."},{"q":"","options":["The house; which her grandfather built in 1960, still stands.","The house, which her grandfather built in 1960 still stands.","The house which her grandfather built in 1960, still stands.","The house, which her grandfather built in 1960, still stands."],"answer":"The house, which her grandfather built in 1960, still stands.","explanation":"Non-restrictive which-clause.","explanation_ar":"جملة which غير المقيِّدة."},{"q":"","options":["Having lived, in three countries Lina speaks four languages.","Having lived in three countries, Lina speaks four languages.","Having lived in three countries; Lina speaks four languages.","Having lived in three countries. Lina speaks four languages."],"answer":"Having lived in three countries, Lina speaks four languages.","explanation":"Introductory participle phrase.","explanation_ar":"العبارة التمهيدية."},{"q":"For many third culture kids, childhood had only one ______ the language that their parents spoke at home.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["constant:","constant;","constant, and","constant."],"answer":"constant:","explanation":"Joining independent clauses (; : , and).","explanation_ar":"ربط الجمل المستقلة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS4-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the boundary error.","items":[{"q":"She had lived abroad for ten years, she still felt Saudi.","options":[],"answer":"She had lived abroad for ten years, but she still felt Saudi.","explanation":"years, she → years, but she. Comma splice or run-on.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: years, she → years, but she. الربط الخاطئ."},{"q":"When I returned home everything felt different.","options":[],"answer":"When I returned home, everything felt different.","explanation":"home everything → home, everything. Dependent clause + comma + main clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: home everything → home, everything. التابعة ثم المستقلة."},{"q":"Since my grandparents spoke only Arabic. I practiced every day.","options":[],"answer":"Since my grandparents spoke only Arabic, I practiced every day.","explanation":"Since my grandparents spoke only Arabic. I → Since my grandparents spoke only Arabic, I. Fragment.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Since my grandparents spoke only Arabic. I → Since my grandparents spoke only Arabic, I. الجملة الناقصة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the boundary error.","items":[{"q":"My cousin grew up in London she now works in Riyadh.","options":[],"answer":"My cousin grew up in London; she now works in Riyadh.","explanation":"London she → London; she. Comma splice or run-on.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: London she → London; she. الربط الخاطئ."},{"q":"The poem which my mother taught me is about the desert at night.","options":[],"answer":"The poem, which my mother taught me, is about the desert at night.","explanation":"which → , which. Non-restrictive which-clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح:  which → , which. جملة which غير المقيِّدة."},{"q":"Having studied abroad. Hamad understands both cultures well.","options":[],"answer":"Having studied abroad, Hamad understands both cultures well.","explanation":"Having studied abroad. Hamad → Having studied abroad, Hamad. Introductory participle phrase.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Having studied abroad. Hamad → Having studied abroad, Hamad. العبارة التمهيدية."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the personal essay.","items":[{"q":"When my family moved to Toronto I was eight years old.","options":[],"answer":"When my family moved to Toronto, I was eight years old.","explanation":"I → , I. Dependent clause + comma + main clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح:  I → , I. التابعة ثم المستقلة."},{"q":"At first, I felt like a fish out of water, I could not understand the jokes my classmates told. [Although I learned English quickly.","options":[],"answer":"At first, I felt like a fish out of water; I could not understand the jokes my classmates told. [Although I learned English quickly.","explanation":"water, I → water; I. Dependent clause + comma + main clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: water, I → water; I. التابعة ثم المستقلة."},{"q":"Although I learned English quickly. I","options":[],"answer":"Although I learned English quickly, I","explanation":"Although I learned English quickly. I → Although I learned English quickly, I. Dependent clause + comma + main clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Although I learned English quickly. I → Although I learned English quickly, I. التابعة ثم المستقلة."},{"q":"Our apartment which overlooked a frozen lake, was very different from our home in Taif.","options":[],"answer":"Our apartment, which overlooked a frozen lake, was very different from our home in Taif.","explanation":"which → , which. Dependent clause + comma + main clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح:  which → , which. التابعة ثم المستقلة."},{"q":"Our apartment, which overlooked a frozen lake was very different from our home in Taif.","options":[],"answer":"Our apartment, which overlooked a frozen lake, was very different from our home in Taif.","explanation":"was → , was. Dependent clause + comma + main clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح:  was → , was. التابعة ثم المستقلة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write correctly punctuated complex sentences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence beginning with a dependent clause (although, when, because).","options":[],"answer":"Although I was born in Riyadh, I spent my childhood in Abha.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses punctuating boundaries in long sentences: introductory dependent clause + comma + main clause; no comma when the dependent clause follows (except contrast with although / whereas); fragments (a dependent clause or participle phrase alone); comma + coordinating conjunction between independent clauses; semicolons and colons between independent clauses; non-restrictive which-clauses; choosing the boundary in SAT questions by identifying clauses on both sides correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write two independent clauses joined by a semicolon.","options":[],"answer":"My father speaks four languages; my mother speaks three.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses punctuating boundaries in long sentences: introductory dependent clause + comma + main clause; no comma when the dependent clause follows (except contrast with although / whereas); fragments (a dependent clause or participle phrase alone); comma + coordinating conjunction between independent clauses; semicolons and colons between independent clauses; non-restrictive which-clauses; choosing the boundary in SAT questions by identifying clauses on both sides correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with a non-restrictive which-clause.","options":[],"answer":"Our old house, which had a huge palm tree in the yard, was sold last year.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses punctuating boundaries in long sentences: introductory dependent clause + comma + main clause; no comma when the dependent clause follows (except contrast with although / whereas); fragments (a dependent clause or participle phrase alone); comma + coordinating conjunction between independent clauses; semicolons and colons between independent clauses; non-restrictive which-clauses; choosing the boundary in SAT questions by identifying clauses on both sides correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spelling Relay","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["Teams line up facing the board.","The teacher says a word; the first student runs to the board and writes the first letter.","The next student adds the next letter, and so on.","The first team with the correct spelling wins the point."],"data":[{"word":"belonging","clue":"the feeling of being accepted as part of a group or place"},{"word":"cosmopolitan","clue":"including people and influences from many countries"},{"word":"ambivalent","clue":"having mixed or opposite feelings about something"},{"word":"diaspora","clue":"people who have left their homeland and live in many other countries"},{"word":"nostalgia","clue":"a feeling of sadness mixed with pleasure when you remember the past"},{"word":"authenticity","clue":"the quality of being real and true to its origins"},{"word":"assimilate","clue":"to become part of a group or culture by adopting its customs"},{"word":"hybrid","clue":"mixing two or more different things"},{"word":"multifaceted","clue":"having many different aspects or sides"},{"word":"allegiance","clue":"loyalty to a country, group or person"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["When we visited my grandfather's village, everyone welcomed us.","When we visited my grandfather's village everyone, welcomed us.","When we visited my grandfather's village. Everyone welcomed us.","When we visited; my grandfather's village everyone welcomed us."],"answer":"When we visited my grandfather's village, everyone welcomed us.","explanation":"Dependent clause + comma + main clause.","explanation_ar":"التابعة ثم المستقلة."},{"q":"","options":["She speaks Arabic at home she thinks in English at school.","She speaks Arabic at home, but she thinks in English at school.","She speaks Arabic at home, she thinks in English at school.","She speaks Arabic at home but, she thinks in English at school."],"answer":"She speaks Arabic at home, but she thinks in English at school.","explanation":"Comma splice or run-on.","explanation_ar":"الربط الخاطئ."},{"q":"","options":["Whereas my brother loves city life; I prefer the quiet of the village.","Whereas my brother loves city life. I prefer the quiet of the village.","Whereas, my brother loves city life I prefer the quiet of the village.","Whereas my brother loves city life, I prefer the quiet of the village."],"answer":"Whereas my brother loves city life, I prefer the quiet of the village.","explanation":"Fragment.","explanation_ar":"الجملة الناقصة."},{"q":"","options":["My grandmother has: one rule we speak only Arabic at dinner.","My grandmother has one rule we speak only Arabic at dinner.","My grandmother has one rule, we speak only Arabic at dinner.","My grandmother has one rule: we speak only Arabic at dinner."],"answer":"My grandmother has one rule: we speak only Arabic at dinner.","explanation":"Joining independent clauses (; : , and).","explanation_ar":"ربط الجمل المستقلة."},{"q":"","options":["Torn between two cultures, the narrator writes letters she never sends.","Torn, between two cultures the narrator writes letters she never sends.","Torn between two cultures. The narrator writes letters she never sends.","Torn between two cultures; the narrator writes letters she never sends."],"answer":"Torn between two cultures, the narrator writes letters she never sends.","explanation":"Introductory participle phrase.","explanation_ar":"العبارة التمهيدية."},{"q":"","options":["The festival which takes place every spring, celebrates the region's poetry.","The festival, which takes place every spring celebrates the region's poetry.","The festival, which takes place every spring, celebrates the region's poetry.","The festival; which takes place every spring; celebrates the region's poetry."],"answer":"The festival, which takes place every spring, celebrates the region's poetry.","explanation":"Non-restrictive which-clause.","explanation_ar":"جملة which غير المقيِّدة."},{"q":"After spending fifteen years in the diaspora, the poet returned to her hometown in ______ her first collection, written entirely in her local dialect, won a national prize.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["2019, two years later,","2019 two years later","2019; two years later,","2019, two years later"],"answer":"2019; two years later,","explanation":"Joining independent clauses (; : , and).","explanation_ar":"ربط الجمل المستقلة."},{"q":"Researchers who study bilingual families have found that children often switch languages depending on the ______ use one language with grandparents and another with friends.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["situation, they may","situation; they may","situation, they, may","situation they may"],"answer":"situation; they may","explanation":"Comma splice or run-on.","explanation_ar":"الربط الخاطئ."},{"q":"Although many young people move to large cities for work and ______ return to their home villages during the holidays to stay connected with their families.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["study. Most","study most,","study; most","study, most"],"answer":"study, most","explanation":"Dependent clause + comma + main clause.","explanation_ar":"التابعة ثم المستقلة."},{"q":"Which of these is a fragment?","options":["Snow was new to her.","She was excited because she had never seen snow before.","Because she had never seen snow before.","She had never seen snow before."],"answer":"Because she had never seen snow before.","explanation":"Fragment.","explanation_ar":"الجملة الناقصة."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Identity in a Global Age” that shows punctuating boundaries in long sentences: introductory dependent clause + comma + main clause; no comma when the dependent clause follows (except contrast with although / whereas); fragments (a dependent clause or participle phrase alone); comma + coordinating conjunction between independent clauses; semicolons and colons between independent clauses; non-restrictive which-clauses; choosing the boundary in SAT questions by identifying clauses on both sides in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Identity in a Global Age” that shows punctuating boundaries in long sentences: introductory dependent clause + comma + main clause; no comma when the dependent clause follows (except contrast with although / whereas); fragments (a dependent clause or participle phrase alone); comma + coordinating conjunction between independent clauses; semicolons and colons between independent clauses; non-restrictive which-clauses; choosing the boundary in SAT questions by identifying clauses on both sides in action"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spelling Relay”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spelling Relay"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Although She Had Lived Abroad, She…: Boundaries in Complex Sentences (SAT)”. Let’s learn punctuating boundaries in long sentences: introductory dependent clause + comma + main clause; no comma when the dependent clause follows (except contrast with although / whereas); fragments (a dependent clause or participle phrase alone); comma + coordinating conjunction between independent clauses; semicolons and colons between independent clauses; non-restrictive which-clauses; choosing the boundary in SAT questions by identifying clauses on both sides together. Start by labeling the clauses on each side of the boundary. An independent clause can stand alone (She still dreamed in Arabic). A dependent clause begins with a subordinator (although, because, when, since, while, if, unless, after, whereas) or a relative word (who, which, that) and cannot stand alone. A dependent clause by itself is a fragment: (not: Because her family moved so often.) Start by labeling the clauses on each side of the boundary. An independent clause can stand alone (She still dreamed in Arabic). A dependent clause begins with a subordinator (although, because, when, since, while, if, unless, after, whereas) or a relative word (who, which, that) and cannot stand alone. A dependent clause by itself is a fragment: (not: Because her family moved so often.) Dependent + independent → comma: Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic. Independent + dependent → usually no comma: She still dreamed in Arabic although she had lived abroad. (A comma is acceptable before although / whereas / while when they show contrast.) Independent + independent → comma + and / but / so / yet / or; or a semicolon; or a colon (if the second explains the first); or a period. Never a comma alone (comma splice) or nothing at all (run-on). Here are some examples. Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic. She still dreamed in Arabic although she had lived abroad for years. Her family moved often; she attended six schools in ten years. Be careful! Some learners say: “Because her family moved so often. She learned to make friends quickly.” That’s not right. We say: “Because her family moved so often, she learned to make friends quickly.” Fragment → attach it. Quick check: \"Because her family moved so often.\" Is this a complete sentence? … Think! … No – it's a fragment. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Although she had lived abroad for years, she still dreamed in Arabic.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS5","title":"Where Are You From?: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to speak reflectively about their own identity.","Students will be able to write a reflective personal essay with fronting, rich vocabulary and correctly punctuated complex sentences, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can talk about what shapes my identity.","I can write a reflective personal essay."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من التحدث بتأمل عن هويتهم.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة مقال شخصي تأملي بالتقديم والمفردات الغنية وجمل معقدة صحيحة الترقيم، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أتحدث عمّا يشكّل هويتي.","أستطيع أن أكتب مقالًا شخصيًّا تأمليًّا."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"belonging","pos":"noun","ipa":"/bɪˈlɔŋɪŋ/","definition":"the feeling of being accepted as part of a group or place","arabic":"الانتماء","example":"Sports clubs give young people a sense of belonging.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"assimilate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əˈsɪməˌleɪt/","definition":"to become part of a group or culture by adopting its customs","arabic":"يندمج / يستوعب","example":"Immigrants often assimilate within two generations.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"cosmopolitan","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌkɑzməˈpɑlətən/","definition":"including people and influences from many countries","arabic":"عالمي الطابع / متعدد الثقافات","example":"Dubai is a cosmopolitan city.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"ambivalent","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/æmˈbɪvələnt/","definition":"having mixed or opposite feelings about something","arabic":"متردد / متضارب المشاعر","example":"She felt ambivalent about moving abroad.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"hybrid","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈhaɪbrəd/","definition":"mixing two or more different things","arabic":"هجين / مختلط","example":"He grew up with a hybrid identity, part Saudi and part British.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"diaspora","pos":"noun","ipa":"/daɪˈæspɚə/","definition":"people who have left their homeland and live in many other countries","arabic":"الشتات / الجاليات في المهجر","example":"The Lebanese diaspora is spread across the world.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"nostalgia","pos":"noun","ipa":"/nɔˈstældʒə/","definition":"a feeling of sadness mixed with pleasure when you remember the past","arabic":"الحنين إلى الماضي","example":"The old songs filled him with nostalgia.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"authenticity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɔθənˈtɪsɪti/","definition":"the quality of being real and true to its origins","arabic":"الأصالة","example":"Tourists value the authenticity of local food.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"multifaceted","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌmʌltiˈfæsətɪd/","definition":"having many different aspects or sides","arabic":"متعدد الأوجه","example":"Identity is a multifaceted concept.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"allegiance","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈlidʒəns/","definition":"loyalty to a country, group or person","arabic":"ولاء","example":"He felt allegiance to both of his countries.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"uprooted","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/əˈprutɪd/","definition":"forced to leave the place where you have lived for a long time","arabic":"مقتلع من جذوره","example":"The war left thousands of families uprooted.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"homogenize","pos":"verb","ipa":"/hoʊˈmɑdʒəˌnaɪz/","definition":"to make things the same or similar","arabic":"يجانس / يوحّد","example":"Some fear that globalization will homogenize cultures.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"juxtapose","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˌdʒʌkstəˈpoʊz/","definition":"to place different things side by side to show contrast","arabic":"يقارن بالتجاور / يضع جنبًا إلى جنب","example":"The exhibition juxtaposes ancient and modern art.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"ancestry","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈænsɛstri/","definition":"your family's origins in the past","arabic":"النسب / الأصل","example":"Many people take DNA tests to learn about their ancestry.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"conformity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈfɔrməti/","definition":"behavior that follows the rules or customs of a group","arabic":"الامتثال / المسايرة","example":"Teenagers often feel pressure toward conformity.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 1: fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion, identity vocabulary and idioms, and boundaries in complex sentences.\nA reflective personal essay opens with a hook (a question or a striking statement), tells a short story about an experience, reflects on what it taught you, and ends with a clear insight. Fronting and inversion give key sentences emphasis; complex sentences show the connections between events and feelings.\n✔ Little did I know… • Never have I… • This much I know: …\n✔ Gone are the days… • Particularly precious are…\n✔ belonging, ancestry, multifaceted, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds\n✔ Although…, … • …; … • …: …","examples":["Although I was born in Taif, I grew up in Toronto.","Little did I know that those years would change me.","Gone are the days when I felt I had to choose.","Particularly precious to me are my grandmother's stories.","At first, I felt like a fish out of water.","Those years gave me the best of both worlds.","This much I know: my identity is multifaceted.","However, I sometimes feel nostalgic for the snow."],"common_errors":["✗ Little I knew that those years would change me. → ✓ Little did I know that those years would change me. (Inversion.)","✗ Although I was born in Taif. I grew up in Toronto. → ✓ Although I was born in Taif, I grew up in Toronto. (Fragment.)","✗ Gone the days are when I felt confused. → ✓ Gone are the days when I felt confused. (Full-verb inversion.)"],"arabic":"نراجع التقديم والقلب للتوكيد، والقلب مع الفعل الرئيس، ومفردات الهوية وتعابيرها، وحدود الجمل المعقدة.\nالمقال الشخصي التأملي يبدأ بمدخل جذاب، ويحكي تجربة قصيرة، ويتأمل ما تعلّمه الكاتب منها، وينتهي بفكرة واضحة.\nالتقديم والقلب يعطيان الجمل الرئيسة قوة، والجمل المعقدة توضّح العلاقة بين الأحداث والمشاعر."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Where Are You From?: Language in Use”. Let’s learn fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L12-U1-LS5 | Where Are You From?: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Identity circle: in groups, each student brings one object, photo or word that represents part of their identity. Speak for one minute about it, using one fronted or inverted sentence (This much I know…, Gone are the days…, Never have I…), two identity words or idioms and one complex sentence beginning with although or when.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 1: fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion, identity vocabulary and idioms, and boundaries in complex sentences. A reflective personal essay opens with a hook (a question or a striking statement), tells a short story about an experience, reflects on what it taught you, and ends with a clear insight. Fronting and inversion give key sentences emphasis; complex sentences show the connections between events and feelings.","on_screen":"✔ Little did I know… • Never have I… • This much I know: … • ✔ Gone are the days… • Particularly precious are… • ✔ belonging, ancestry, multifaceted, a fish out of water, the best of both worlds","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Although I was born in Taif, I grew up in Toronto. Little did I know that those years would change me. Gone are the days when I felt I had to choose.","on_screen":"Although I was born in Taif, I grew up in Toronto. / Little did I know that those years would change me. / Gone are the days when I felt I had to choose.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Little I knew that those years would change me.” That’s not right. We say: “Little did I know that those years would change me.” Inversion.","on_screen":"✗ Little I knew that those years would change me.   ✓ Little did I know that those years would change me.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “Although I was born in Taif. I grew up in Toronto.”? … Think! … It should be “Although I was born in Taif, I grew up in Toronto.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can talk about what shapes my identity.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can talk about what shapes my identity.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع التقديم والقلب للتوكيد، والقلب مع الفعل الرئيس، ومفردات الهوية وتعابيرها، وحدود الجمل المعقدة.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Little I knew that those years would change me. — الصحيح: Little did I know that those years would change me.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أتحدث عمّا يشكّل هويتي."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Where Are You From? – Lina","body":"\"Where are you from?\" is a question I have never been able to answer in a single word.\nAlthough I was born in Taif, I spent most of my childhood in Toronto, where my father was studying.\nAt first, I felt like a fish out of water; I could not even understand my classmates' jokes.\nLittle did I know that those difficult years would give me the best of both worlds.\nGone are the days when I felt I had to choose between two cultures.\nParticularly precious to me are my grandmother's stories, which connect me to my ancestry.\nWhen I speak English, I feel confident and curious; when I speak Arabic, I feel at home.\nThis much I know: my identity is multifaceted, and I would not change any part of it.","word_count":133,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Lina's essay. True or False?","items":[{"q":"Lina can easily answer \"Where are you from?\" in one word.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Where Are You From? – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"She was born in Taif but spent most of her childhood in Toronto.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Where Are You From? – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"At first, she understood all her classmates' jokes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Where Are You From? – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Lina believes the difficult years gave her the best of both worlds.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Where Are You From? – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"She still feels she must choose between two cultures.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Where Are You From? – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Her grandmother's stories connect her to her ancestry.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Where Are You From? – Lina”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Little ___ that those difficult years would give me the best of both worlds.","options":["knew I","did I know","I did know","I knew"],"answer":"did I know","explanation":"Fronting or inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم والقلب."},{"q":"___ the days when I felt I had to choose between two cultures.","options":["Gone are","Gone is","The gone","Are gone"],"answer":"Gone are","explanation":"Fronting or inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم والقلب."},{"q":"At first, I felt like a fish out of ___.","options":["the sea","the bowl","water","the lake"],"answer":"water","explanation":"Identity vocabulary or idiom.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الهوية."},{"q":"","options":["Although I was born in Taif, I spent most of my childhood in Toronto.","Although I was born in Taif. I spent most of my childhood in Toronto.","Although I was born in Taif; I spent most of my childhood in Toronto.","Although, I was born in Taif I spent most of my childhood in Toronto."],"answer":"Although I was born in Taif, I spent most of my childhood in Toronto.","explanation":"Boundary punctuation.","explanation_ar":"ترقيم الحدود."},{"q":"Particularly precious to me ___ my grandmother's stories.","options":["do","has","is","are"],"answer":"are","explanation":"Fronting or inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم والقلب."},{"q":"The first months in a new country were lonely and confusing. ______ by the end of the year, I had made close friends and begun to feel at home.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For example,","Similarly,","However,","Therefore,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Plan your identity talk or essay.","items":[{"q":"Your object, photo or word and what it represents: ___","options":[],"answer":"My grandfather's old radio represents the stories of our village.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence with negative fronting: ___","options":[],"answer":"Never have I felt as proud as when I recited his poem at school.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence beginning \"Gone are…\" or \"Particularly… is / are…\": ___","options":[],"answer":"Particularly important to me is the dialect of my village.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Two identity words or idioms: ___","options":[],"answer":"I feel a strong sense of belonging, and I sometimes feel nostalgic.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A complex sentence beginning with \"When\" or \"Although\": ___","options":[],"answer":"When I hear the old songs, I remember my childhood summers.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your reflective essay.","items":[{"q":"Plan: hook, experience, feelings, reflection, insight.","options":[],"answer":"Hook: \"Which language do you dream in?\"; experience: moving from Jazan to Riyadh; feelings: a fish out of water; reflection: both dialects are mine; insight: identity grows","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your reflective essay answering the question you chose (8–9 sentences).","options":[],"answer":"\"Which language do you dream in?\" a friend once asked me, and I could not answer. When my family moved from Jazan to Riyadh, I was eleven years old. At first, I felt like a fish out of water; my classmates laughed at my southern dialect. Little did I know that this experience would teach me to value my roots. Gone are the days when I tried to hide the way I spoke. Particularly important to me now are the songs my grandmother sang in our village dialect. Although I speak like a Riyadh native at school, I still switch to my dialect at home. This much I know: my identity is multifaceted, and every part of it belongs to me.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: inversion after negative fronting? full-verb inversion after \"Gone\"? no fragments or comma splices? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"Although I speak like a Riyadh native at school. I still switch\" to \"Although I speak like a Riyadh native at school, I still switch\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Board Race Review","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["One student from each team stands at the board.","The teacher says a cue word and a task (meaning, spelling or sentence with fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review)).","The first correct answer wins a point; rotate players every round.","Play 10 rounds."],"data":[{"cue":"belonging","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"assimilate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"cosmopolitan","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"ambivalent","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"hybrid","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"diaspora","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"nostalgia","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"authenticity","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"multifaceted","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"allegiance","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"uprooted","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"homogenize","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"juxtapose","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"ancestry","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"conformity","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Never ___ as proud as when I recited my grandfather's poem.","options":["felt I have","I have felt","have I felt","I felt"],"answer":"have I felt","explanation":"Fronting or inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم والقلب."},{"q":"Into the courtyard ___ my grandmother, carrying a tray of dates.","options":["walking","she walked","walked","did walk"],"answer":"walked","explanation":"Fronting or inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم والقلب."},{"q":"Many children of migrants have a foot in both ___.","options":["camps","sides","houses","fields"],"answer":"camps","explanation":"Identity vocabulary or idiom.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الهوية."},{"q":"Some proverbs get lost in ___ because they depend on local culture.","options":["transformation","transition","transmission","translation"],"answer":"translation","explanation":"Identity vocabulary or idiom.","explanation_ar":"مفردات الهوية."},{"q":"","options":["My family has lived in Taif for generations, my roots are here.","My family has lived in Taif for generations; and my roots are here.","My family has lived in Taif for generations my roots are here.","My family has lived in Taif for generations; my roots are here."],"answer":"My family has lived in Taif for generations; my roots are here.","explanation":"Boundary punctuation.","explanation_ar":"ترقيم الحدود."},{"q":"Many young Saudis who studied abroad say they gained independence and confidence. ______ some admit that returning home required a period of readjustment.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Therefore,","As a result,","At the same time,","For instance,"],"answer":"At the same time,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"Sadu weaving was traditionally practiced by Bedouin women. ______ young designers today use Sadu patterns in fashion, furniture and digital art.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Today, however,","Therefore,","For example,","Similarly,"],"answer":"Today, however,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• \"Third culture kids\" grow up in a country that is not their parents' homeland.\n• The term was coined by sociologist Ruth Hill Useem in the 1950s.\n• Such children often adapt easily to new places.\n• Some struggle to answer the question \"Where are you from?\"\n\nThe student wants to present both an advantage and a challenge of being a third culture kid. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["The term was coined in the 1950s.","Although third culture kids often adapt easily to new places, some struggle to answer the question \"Where are you from?\"","Third culture kids grow up abroad.","Ruth Hill Useem was a sociologist."],"answer":"Although third culture kids often adapt easily to new places, some struggle to answer the question \"Where are you from?\"","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Al-Sadu is a traditional form of weaving in Saudi Arabia.\n• It uses wool from sheep, camels and goats.\n• It is known for its geometric patterns.\n• UNESCO recognized it as intangible cultural heritage in 2020.\n\nThe student wants to introduce Al-Sadu to an audience unfamiliar with it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["It uses wool.","Al-Sadu, a traditional form of Saudi weaving known for its geometric patterns, uses wool from sheep, camels and goats.","Many countries have weaving traditions.","UNESCO recognized it in 2020."],"answer":"Al-Sadu, a traditional form of Saudi weaving known for its geometric patterns, uses wool from sheep, camels and goats.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best opening sentence for a reflective personal essay:","options":["\"Where are you from?\" is a question I have never been able to answer in a single word.","I'm from Taif, I'm from Toronto, I'm from everywhere it's complicated.","Where am I from. I don't know.","Little I knew that I am from two places."],"answer":"\"Where are you from?\" is a question I have never been able to answer in a single word.","explanation":"Fronting or inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم والقلب."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A university application workshop: Lina writing a personal essay titled \"Where Are You From?\" on a laptop; a corkboard with photos of Taif, Toronto and her grandmother's hands weaving Sadu; sticky notes with \"belonging\", \"nostalgia\", \"the best of both worlds\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A university application workshop: Lina writing a personal essay titled \"Where Are You From?\" on a laptop; a corkboard with photos of Taif, Toronto and her grandmother's hands weaving Sadu; sticky notes with \"belonging\", \"nostalgia\", \"the best of both worlds\""},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Identity in a Global Age”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Identity in a Global Age”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L12-U1-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Board Race Review”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Board Race Review"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS5-T1","script":"\"Where are you from?\" is a question I have never been able to answer in a single word. Although I was born in Taif, I spent most of my childhood in Toronto, where my father was studying. At first, I felt like a fish out of water; I could not even understand my classmates' jokes. Little did I know that those difficult years would give me the best of both worlds. Gone are the days when I felt I had to choose between two cultures. Particularly precious to me are my grandmother's stories, which connect me to my ancestry. When I speak English, I feel confident and curious; when I speak Arabic, I feel at home. This much I know: my identity is multifaceted, and I would not change any part of it.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L12-U1-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Where Are You From?: Language in Use”. Let’s learn fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials, abstract and idiomatic identity vocabulary, boundaries in complex sentences; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Identity circle: in groups, each student brings one object, photo or word that represents part of their identity. Speak for one minute about it, using one fronted or inverted sentence (This much I know…, Gone are the days…, Never have I…), two identity words or idioms and one complex sentence beginning with although or when. Today you use all of Unit 1: fronting and inversion for emphasis, full-verb inversion, identity vocabulary and idioms, and boundaries in complex sentences. A reflective personal essay opens with a hook (a question or a striking statement), tells a short story about an experience, reflects on what it taught you, and ends with a clear insight. Fronting and inversion give key sentences emphasis; complex sentences show the connections between events and feelings. Here are some examples. Although I was born in Taif, I grew up in Toronto. Little did I know that those years would change me. Gone are the days when I felt I had to choose. Be careful! Some learners say: “Little I knew that those years would change me.” That’s not right. We say: “Little did I know that those years would change me.” Inversion. Quick check: what is wrong with “Although I was born in Taif. I grew up in Toronto.”? … Think! … It should be “Although I was born in Taif, I grew up in Toronto.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can talk about what shapes my identity.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"That moment ___.","options":["never I will forget","will I never forget","I never will forget it","I will never forget"],"answer":"I will never forget","explanation":"Fronted object or complement (no inversion).","explanation_ar":"تقديم المفعول به."},{"q":"Little ___ how much their lives would change.","options":["did they realize","they did realize","they realized","realized they"],"answer":"did they realize","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"So strongly ___ about her heritage that she wrote a book about it.","options":["did she feel","she felt","felt she","she did feel"],"answer":"did she feel","explanation":"So / Such + inversion.","explanation_ar":"So / Such."},{"q":"Rarely, if ever, ___ such a diverse group of students in one class.","options":["I have taught","did I taught","taught I have","have I taught"],"answer":"have I taught","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"More valuable than any souvenir ___ the friendships we made.","options":["are","do","have","is"],"answer":"are","explanation":"Fronted comparative / adjective + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم المقارنة."},{"q":"Not until she returned from her studies abroad ______ how much her grandmother's stories had shaped her view of the world.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["she did realize","realized she","did she realize","she realized"],"answer":"did she realize","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"___ the days when a phone call abroad cost a fortune.","options":["Gone is","It's gone","Are gone","Gone are"],"answer":"Gone are","explanation":"Gone are / Gone is…","explanation_ar":"Gone are the days."},{"q":"Particularly moving ___ the letters written by the first students who studied abroad.","options":["has","does","are","is"],"answer":"are","explanation":"Fronted adjective + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم الصفة."},{"q":"On the edge of the oasis ___ a mud-brick fort.","options":["standing","stands","does stand","it stands"],"answer":"stands","explanation":"Place / direction adverbial + verb + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم ظرف المكان."},{"q":"Look! Here ___!","options":["come they","they come","do they come","they do come"],"answer":"they come","explanation":"No inversion with pronoun subjects.","explanation_ar":"الضمير لا يُقلب."},{"q":"Included in the exhibition ______ several photographs taken by the first Saudi woman to work as a professional photographer in the region.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["are","does","it is","is"],"answer":"are","explanation":"Included / Attached / Enclosed + be + subject.","explanation_ar":"تقديم اسم المفعول."},{"q":"At my new school, I felt like a fish out of ___.","options":["the river","water","its tank","the sea"],"answer":"water","explanation":"Identity idiom.","explanation_ar":"التعبير الاصطلاحي."},{"q":"She felt ___ about leaving: excited but also sad.","options":["cosmopolitan","ambivalent","uprooted","homogenized"],"answer":"ambivalent","explanation":"Identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The museum's ___ comes from its use of original materials.","options":["authentically","authenticate","authenticity","authentic"],"answer":"authenticity","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"Having grown up partly in Riyadh and partly in Paris, the designer describes her style as ______, combining Saudi embroidery with French tailoring.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["homogeneous","conformist","hybrid","uprooted"],"answer":"hybrid","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"My grandfather often speaks ___ about the village where he grew up.","options":["nostalgic","nostalgically","nostalgia","nostalgist"],"answer":"nostalgically","explanation":"Word-family form.","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"","options":["Although, she was born abroad she speaks perfect Arabic.","Although she was born abroad, she speaks perfect Arabic.","Although she was born abroad. She speaks perfect Arabic.","Although she was born abroad; she speaks perfect Arabic."],"answer":"Although she was born abroad, she speaks perfect Arabic.","explanation":"Dependent clause + comma + main clause.","explanation_ar":"التابعة ثم المستقلة."},{"q":"","options":["The village which my father left in 1985, has changed completely.","The village; which my father left in 1985, has changed completely.","The village, which my father left in 1985 has changed completely.","The village, which my father left in 1985, has changed completely."],"answer":"The village, which my father left in 1985, has changed completely.","explanation":"Non-restrictive which-clause.","explanation_ar":"جملة which غير المقيِّدة."},{"q":"Many of the families who moved to the city in the 1980s kept close ties with their home ______ returned every summer for weddings, harvests and religious holidays.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["villages, they","villages, and, they","villages; they","villages they"],"answer":"villages; they","explanation":"Joining independent clauses (; : , and).","explanation_ar":"ربط الجمل المستقلة."},{"q":"Some people fear that globalization will make every city look the same. ______ many cities are now investing in the protection of their historic districts and local traditions.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","Therefore,","For example,","Similarly,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Reverse culture shock is the difficulty people feel when they return home after living abroad.\n• Familiar places may suddenly seem strange.\n• Friends and family may not understand their experiences.\n• The feeling usually fades within a few months.\n\nThe student wants to define reverse culture shock for an unfamiliar audience. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["The feeling usually fades within a few months.","Friends may not understand.","Many people live abroad.","Reverse culture shock is the difficulty people feel when they return home after living abroad and find that familiar places suddenly seem strange."],"answer":"Reverse culture shock is the difficulty people feel when they return home after living abroad and find that familiar places suddenly seem strange.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"little i knew that gone the days were, when i felt confused.\"","options":["Little did I know that gone the days were when I felt confused.","little did I know that the days when I felt confused were gone.","Little I knew that the days when I felt confused were gone.","Little did I know that the days when I felt confused were gone."],"answer":"Little did I know that the days when I felt confused were gone.","explanation":"Negative or restrictive fronting + inversion.","explanation_ar":"التقديم المنفي."},{"q":"Which sentence contains a fronted object with no inversion?","options":["This much I can promise you.","Here comes the bus.","Gone are the days of letters.","Never have I seen such courage."],"answer":"This much I can promise you.","explanation":"Fronted object or complement (no inversion).","explanation_ar":"تقديم المفعول به."},{"q":"Which word group cannot stand alone as a sentence?","options":["Having lived in four countries, she adapted easily.","She adapted easily because she had moved so often.","Having lived in four countries before the age of ten.","She had lived in four countries before the age of ten."],"answer":"Having lived in four countries before the age of ten.","explanation":"Fragment.","explanation_ar":"الجملة الناقصة."}],"theme_ar":"الهوية في عصر العولمة","essential_question_ar":"هل يمكن أن ننتمي إلى أكثر من مكان دون أن نفقد هويتنا؟","can_do":"I can front objects, complements and negative adverbials for emphasis, use full-verb inversion after fronted adjectives and adverbials (Gone are the days…, Into the room walked…), discuss identity with abstract and idiomatic vocabulary, and punctuate boundaries in long, complex sentences.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أقدّم المفعول به والمكمّل والظروف المنفية للتوكيد، وأستخدم القلب مع الفعل الرئيس بعد تقديم الصفات والظروف (Gone are the days… / Into the room walked…)، وأناقش الهوية بمفردات مجردة وتعابير اصطلاحية، وأرقّم حدود الجمل الطويلة المعقدة."});
